On Fri, 2014-08-01 at 15:47 -0700, Dennis Murphy wrote:
> Like Doug, it's not clear to me what you really want.

OK, let me try a plain text version of the kind of result I would like:

====================================================================
                Effect Sizes
====================================================================
Effect  |  Mean |  Distribution
A       |  0.5  |  <graph1>
B       |  0.1  |  <graph2>
C       | -0.1  |  <graph3>
....
==================================================================

For each effect I have a bunch of simulated values, which together make
up the estimated distribution of the effect.  The data source for each
effect is different.  I want graph1, for example, to be a smoothed
density plot of the distribution of the effect values for A.

It would be desirable if each graph had the same scale and the reference
point (0) lined up.

> If you want to
> produce densities at levels of a single factor or level combinations
> of two factors, then faceting is what you probably want. OTOH, if you
> want to place inset graphs at selected (spatial) locations, say on a
> map, then you need a different type of solution.

I hope it's clearer now what I'm trying to do; it's not exactly either
of the possibilities you mention.

sparklines looks as if it's in the right direction, but the
documentation for it is pretty minimal.

The table itself will likely be in LaTeX, although ultimately everything
has to end up in MS Word.

My initial idea was that I'd produce a little file for each <graphN>; an
alternative would be to have a single image that I could use to file the
entire "Distribution" column.

Ross

