Dear R-users,

I want to create a marginal effect (ME) plot with a histogram overly with ggplot2 similar to what Berry et al recommends. See e.g. figure 4 in this article https://files.nyu.edu/mrg217/public/jop2.pdf.

My main problem is that I am not getting the scale correct between the histogram and the ME-plot on the y-axis. The code below actually do produce some reasonable scales but many of the other models I have will also have proportional scales. This will mean that either the histogram will be very much squeezed or the ME-plot will be so. So my question is primarily how can one solve this issue by using two y-scales as Berry et al does where the left y-axis is scaled according to the ME plot and the right y-axis scaled according to the histogram as percentage of observations (or count). They use STATA to produce those figures by the way and therefore I cannot see any reason why R cannot do it.

My secondary question would be if one can use one y-scale but somehow weight or standardize the histogram scale according to the ME-scale in order to get the proportions correct.

Part of the problem is that I need to use two different dataframes for the ggplot() since I am using multilevel models. So the distribution of the histogram actually refers to the country level variable and therefore of a different length of the ME dataframe which is called df here.

All input is appreciated
Adel

The code and data follows here below:

#Plotting witht ggplot
library(ggplot2)
df<-data.frame(moderator, ME=marginaleffect(ce,int,moderator), upperbound, lowerbound, zero=0)
# country<-data.frame(QoG=unique(test2$QoG)) #create country level data

# plot ME-plot
p<-ggplot(data=df, aes(x=moderator)) + geom_line(aes(y=ME)) + geom_line(aes(y=upperbound), linetype =2)  + geom_line(aes(y=lowerbound), linetype =2)

#add titles
p<-p+labs(title="DisFre-malnutrition")   + ylab(expression("ME"*(Disaster~"|"~QoG)==partialdiff~deprivation / partialdiff~disaster)) + xlab("QoG \n 90 % confidence interval") + geom_line(aes(y=zero), colour = "black")

#add histogram
plot.Dis_malnut<-p+geom_histogram(data=country, aes(x=unique(country$QoG)), binwidth=0.2, fill="grey", alpha = 0.5)

